A||||4||rnoswal||larger rims||||1||
Z||000000||rnoswal||10-10-2000||11:49 PM||||I have an 85 2.5 and would like to put larger rims on it. How much larger can you go without hitting the lower spring support on the rear suspension? I've seen some really nice rims on some Fiero's, but couldn't see how they could fit them. Anyone?<P>Russ<P>------------------<BR>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000001||SibERThuG||10-11-2000||12:29 AM||||Go to tirerack.com and go through the "wheels" section. They will only select the wheels that will fit. I think it can go up to 17inch wheels. I know I can swallow 17's on my 88' formula. Also, if you put low-profile tires on, you can go even bigger. Another thing--- if your car is lowered/chopped, you must also include this as a factor.<P>------------------<BR>Robbie<BR>88' Formula||||reg||12
Z||000002||1FST2M6||10-11-2000||08:15 AM||||18s will fit with a 45mm offset.. 19s will fit but only pirelli makes a 225-35-19 and at $385 each its not worth the "cool factor" i've got 17x7up front and 17x8.5 in the back. with no problems. <BR>this might help a bit...  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||msweldon||10-11-2000||09:20 AM||||I too have a similar setup as 1FST2M6, except I run 16's up front.  I run a 17X9 in the rear with 255/40/17's and have about 1/2 inch clearance between the strut and tire.  You could purchase a set of rear coilovers from RCC and go crazy with your rim height.  I once had these on my car and was able to put a 265/40/17 back there and there was still plenty of room.<P>hope this helps,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000004||HYPTNOTiSE||10-11-2000||09:32 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/hyptnotise/graphics/entire.jpg">  <P>Those are 18'' wheels with 225/40 series tires.<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by HYPTNOTiSE (edited 10-11-2000).]||||reg||1
